# Response headers for the static site on Cloudflare Workers.
# Cloudflare reads this file from the assets directory; vite copies
# everything in web/public/ into web/dist/, so it ships with the build.
#
# The Content-Security-Policy below is also applied by the end-to-end test
# (e2e-run.mts reads THIS file), so a policy that breaks the application
# fails CI instead of reaching the site. Keep it as one line: the parser
# there, and Cloudflare's, both read one header per line.
#
# Why each source is allowed:
#   'wasm-unsafe-eval'  the Rust core is WebAssembly; without it nothing runs
#   blob: in script/worker  ffmpeg.wasm loads its 32 MB core from a blob URL
#   'unsafe-inline' in style  Google Fonts injects a stylesheet; note this
#                       does NOT weaken script, which is where XSS lives
#   data: in img        the favicon is an inline SVG data URI
# There is no 'unsafe-inline' and no 'unsafe-eval' for scripts: the build
# emits one external module and no inline script, so nothing needs them.
# Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy and Cross-Origin-Embedder-Policy isolate the
# origin, which is what unlocks SharedArrayBuffer and with it the
# multithreaded ffmpeg core (public/ffmpeg/mt). require-corp rather than
# credentialless because Safari only knows the former. Every cross-origin
# resource must then be CORS: the Google Fonts stylesheet is loaded with
# crossorigin in index.html, and font files are always fetched with CORS.
/*
  Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy: same-origin
  Cross-Origin-Embedder-Policy: require-corp
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; base-uri 'self'; object-src 'none'; form-action 'none'; frame-ancestors 'none'; script-src 'self' 'wasm-unsafe-eval' blob:; worker-src 'self' blob:; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline' https://fonts.googleapis.com; font-src 'self' https://fonts.gstatic.com; img-src 'self' data: blob:; media-src 'self' blob:; connect-src 'self' blob: data:
  Referrer-Policy: no-referrer
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff

# Everything under /assets/ carries a content hash in its name (vite), so a
# given URL never changes: the browser can keep it for a year and skip the
# revalidation. Without this rule Cloudflare serves the files with
# `max-age=0, must-revalidate`, and every `new Worker()` in the pool was one
# more conditional request to the origin. The document, sw.js and the
# ffmpeg chunks are not hashed and keep the default.
/assets/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable
